WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.321
Picture your program as a page of code.

2
00:00:05.467 --> 00:00:08.801
Something has to walk through it, one line after another.

3
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.035
It keeps its own notes on a stack.

4
00:00:11.035 --> 00:00:15.584
Every call in progress gets a frame there, with that call's local variables.

5
00:00:15.733 --> 00:00:18.512
Step into a call, and a frame goes on top.

6
00:00:18.512 --> 00:00:21.357
When the call returns, its frame comes off.

7
00:00:21.667 --> 00:00:26.448
This walker, with its place in the code and its own stack, is a thread.

8
00:00:26.448 --> 00:00:28.603
The JVM starts this one for you.

9
00:00:28.603 --> 00:00:29.815
It is called main.

10
00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:32.204
It runs inside a process.

11
00:00:32.204 --> 00:00:36.894
A process is a running program with its own memory, like one JVM.

12
00:00:36.894 --> 00:00:39.636
No other process can read that memory.

13
00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:45.695
Objects you create with new, and static fields, live in one part of that memory: the heap.

14
00:00:45.867 --> 00:00:48.167
Now start a second thread, a worker.

15
00:00:48.167 --> 00:00:51.491
It gets its own place in the code and its own stack.

16
00:00:52.067 --> 00:00:54.266
Both threads call the same method.

17
00:00:54.266 --> 00:00:57.243
Each one gets its own frame, on its own stack.

18
00:00:57.467 --> 00:00:59.032
But they share the heap.

19
00:00:59.032 --> 00:01:01.576
Both can reach the same Payment object.

20
00:01:01.733 --> 00:01:03.806
That is what makes threads useful.

21
00:01:03.806 --> 00:01:08.317
One thread puts a payment in a map, and another reads it, with no copying.

22
00:01:08.467 --> 00:01:10.823
It is also what makes them dangerous.

23
00:01:10.823 --> 00:01:14.517
Two threads can change the same object at the same moment.

24
00:01:15.067 --> 00:01:18.503
Now main changes its local variable total to three.

25
00:01:18.503 --> 00:01:20.861
Does the worker's total change too?

26
00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:23.006
No.

27
00:01:23.006 --> 00:01:27.808
Each total lives on its own stack, where no other thread can reach it.

28
00:01:27.808 --> 00:01:29.455
Only the heap is shared.

29
00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:32.956
So what moves a thread along its code?

30
00:01:32.956 --> 00:01:34.167
A core of the CPU.

31
00:01:34.167 --> 00:01:36.521
One core runs one thread at a time.

32
00:01:36.667 --> 00:01:38.134
Two threads, one core.

33
00:01:38.134 --> 00:01:43.336
The scheduler in the operating system lets main run for a short slice of time.

34
00:01:43.533 --> 00:01:47.288
Then it saves main's registers, and switches to the worker.

35
00:01:47.288 --> 00:01:48.879
That is a context switch.

36
00:01:48.879 --> 00:01:51.361
It happens thousands of times a second.

37
00:01:51.533 --> 00:01:55.220
Both threads are in progress, taking turns on one core.

38
00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:56.560
That is concurrency.

39
00:01:56.733 --> 00:02:00.324
Give them two cores, and both run at the same instant.

40
00:02:00.324 --> 00:02:01.653
That is parallelism.

41
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:04.868
Concurrency is about how you split the work.

42
00:02:04.868 --> 00:02:06.891
Parallelism needs real cores.

43
00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:11.735
Now a busy job, all additions, on a machine with four cores.

44
00:02:11.735 --> 00:02:14.555
Is it faster on eight threads than on four?

45
00:02:16.533 --> 00:02:26.311
In one run of the demo in this lesson, it took 510 milliseconds on one thread, 257 on two, and 121 on four.

46
00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:27.996
And 125 on eight.

47
00:02:27.996 --> 00:02:32.843
Once every core is busy, the extra threads only take turns.

48
00:02:33.067 --> 00:02:37.414
A thread that waits for a network reply uses no core while it waits.

49
00:02:37.414 --> 00:02:40.803
So waiting work can use many more threads than cores.

50
00:02:41.733 --> 00:02:44.679
Last question: how do you make a new thread?

51
00:02:44.679 --> 00:02:48.360
Here is main, about to run a task on a thread object t.

52
00:02:48.533 --> 00:02:50.813
Calling run is a plain method call.

53
00:02:50.813 --> 00:02:55.373
Main steps into the task itself, and a frame goes on main's own stack.

54
00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:57.142
No new thread exists.

55
00:02:57.142 --> 00:03:02.504
Nothing fails, your tests pass, and the job is exactly as slow as before.

56
00:03:03.467 --> 00:03:05.330
Calling start is different.

57
00:03:05.330 --> 00:03:10.093
The JVM asks the operating system for a new thread, with a new stack.

58
00:03:10.267 --> 00:03:15.457
The new thread calls run on its own stack, and main goes on to its next line at once.

59
00:03:16.333 --> 00:03:19.196
In code it looks the same in Java and Kotlin.

60
00:03:19.196 --> 00:03:20.659
start makes the thread.

61
00:03:20.659 --> 00:03:23.649
join waits here until that thread has finished.

62
00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:28.415
And run, called by you, runs the task on the thread you are already on.

63
00:03:29.333 --> 00:03:29.917
The rule.

64
00:03:29.917 --> 00:03:35.491
Every thread has its own stack and shares one heap, and only start makes a new thread.

65
00:03:35.667 --> 00:03:36.627
Check yourself.

66
00:03:36.627 --> 00:03:43.410
A report is split into four slices, and you call run on a new thread for each one, on a four-core machine.

67
00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:45.841
How much faster is it than one thread?

68
00:03:48.467 --> 00:03:49.713
Not faster at all.

69
00:03:49.713 --> 00:03:55.669
Each run is a plain call, so main does the four slices one after another, on one core.
